WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.773
Millions of Taylor Swift fans hit Ticketmaster at once.

00:00:05.773 --> 00:00:09.546
The transactions held, no fan got the same seat twice.

00:00:09.546 --> 00:00:13.320
But the site still melted under the load.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.093
Keeping transactions correct and fast at that scale is this module.

00:00:17.093 --> 00:00:19.233
Slow down and watch one fan.

00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:21.373
Alice clicks Buy.

00:00:21.373 --> 00:00:23.513
Now the database has three things to do in two seconds.

00:00:23.513 --> 00:00:25.653
Check that her seat is still open.

00:00:25.653 --> 00:00:27.793
Charge her card.

00:00:27.793 --> 00:00:29.934
Record the sale.

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:32.074
Three steps.

00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:34.214
One decision.

00:00:34.214 --> 00:00:36.354
All three happen, or none of them happen.

00:00:36.354 --> 00:00:38.494
Now run that ten thousand times every second, with everyone reaching for the same seats.

00:00:38.494 --> 00:00:40.634
One fan is easy.

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:42.774
Millions at once is the problem.

00:00:42.774 --> 00:00:46.928
So what actually broke?

00:00:46.928 --> 00:00:51.082
Start with the demand.

00:00:51.082 --> 00:00:55.235
Twelve million visitors and scalper bots threw three and a half billion requests at the site, four times its prior peak.

00:00:55.235 --> 00:00:59.389
Two things could give, correctness or performance.

00:00:59.389 --> 00:01:03.543
Under the load, availability bent: queues froze thousands deep, sales were paused on purpose to stay up, and holds and payments timed out.

00:01:03.543 --> 00:01:07.697
But correctness held.

00:01:07.697 --> 00:01:11.850
No seat sold twice, and a record two point four million tickets sold in a day.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.004
The transactions did their job.

00:01:16.004 --> 00:01:20.158
Delivering them fast, under that crush, did not.

00:01:20.158 --> 00:01:25.047
Here is the thing that should bother you.

00:01:25.047 --> 00:01:29.937
Other systems handle far more and never make headlines.

00:01:29.937 --> 00:01:34.826
Visa clears a hundred thousand transactions a second at peak, every single day.

00:01:34.826 --> 00:01:39.716
Amazon on Prime Day serves a hundred million requests a second, answering in under ten milliseconds.

00:01:39.716 --> 00:01:44.605
Ticketmaster's peak was four times its own prior record, and it fell over.

00:01:44.605 --> 00:01:49.495
The difference is decades of engineering for massive concurrent load that Visa and Amazon paid for, and a one-off flash on-sale had not.

00:01:49.495 --> 00:01:54.384
Size was never the issue.

00:01:54.384 --> 00:01:59.018
Now pull back and look at the long arc.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:03.653
Each generation of this stack is roughly ten thousand times bigger than the last, about a hundred times every decade.

00:02:03.653 --> 00:02:08.287
The classic era ran thousands of transactions a second on spinning disks and B-trees.

00:02:08.287 --> 00:02:12.922
The modern era runs tens of millions a second on solid state and LSM trees.

00:02:12.922 --> 00:02:17.556
Quick question.

00:02:17.556 --> 00:02:22.191
What drives the next jump?

00:02:22.191 --> 00:02:26.825
AI agents.

00:02:26.825 --> 00:02:31.460
Millions of them, plus serverless apps, all hitting the same data at once, on hardware we have not even standardized yet.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:36.094
Orders of magnitude bigger, three generations running, and every one still owes the same correctness the classic era did.

00:02:36.094 --> 00:02:38.236
The rest of this module answers one question.

00:02:38.236 --> 00:02:40.378
How do thousands of transactions run at once, yet each behave as if it ran alone?

00:02:40.378 --> 00:02:42.520
The answer is ACID.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.662
Atomicity.

00:02:44.662 --> 00:02:46.804
Consistency.

00:02:46.804 --> 00:02:48.946
Isolation.

00:02:48.946 --> 00:02:51.088
Durability.

00:02:51.088 --> 00:02:53.230
Next page starts there.
